==English==
{{was wotd|2006|July|18}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|pandar}}

===Etymology===
From Chaucer’s character {{m|en|w:Pandarus|Pandare}} (in ''{{w|Troilus and Criseyde}}'') (see also Shakespeare’s ''{{w|Troilus and Cressida}}''), from {{der|en|it|w:Pandarus|Pandaro}} (found in {{w|Boccaccio}}), from {{der|en|la|w:Pandarus|Pandarus}} (found in {{w|Greek mythology}}), from {{der|en|grc|Πάνδαρος}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈpandə/|lang=en}} or {{IPAchar|/ˈpændə/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈpændɚ/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-pander.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{a|non-rhotic accents}} {{homophones|panda|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A person who furthers the [[illicit]] love-affairs of others; a [[pimp]] or [[procurer]]. (Later [[panderer]].)
#* '''1992''', Moncrieff/Kilmartin/Enright, translating Marcel Proust, ''Swann’s Way'', Folio Society 2005, p. 190:
#*: It was not only the brilliant phalanx of virtuous dowagers, generals and academicians with whom he was most intimately associated that Swann so cynically compelled to serve him as '''panders'''.
# An offer of illicit [[sex]] with a third party.
# An illicit or illegal [[offer]], usually to [[tempt]].
# {{lb|en|by extension}} One who ministers to the evil designs and passions of another.
#* Burke
#*: Those wicked '''panders''' to avarice and ambition.

